From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Priestley <voicedrew11@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 3 Oct 2026 18:41:18 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 13/14] gs: rasterize asynchronously on a pool of worker
 threads, bit-exact

The CPU backend drew every primitive synchronously on the EE thread, from
inside VIF1/VU1 XGKICK processing: in KFIV's 3D areas over half the frame
was rasterization, serialized with everything else.

Submit now appends to a batch of commands that a pool of worker threads
(PS2X_GS_THREADS, default min(8, cores/2)) executes while the next batch
fills. Each worker replays the whole batch but only writes the screen rows
of its 8-row stripes, so every pixel is still written by one thread in
submission order.

- Uploads, CLUT loads and TEXFLUSH are stream commands; each worker keeps
  its own CLUT and texture page cache (dropped at TEXFLUSH, as before).
- Hazards are tracked per 8 KiB page, mirroring GSMem addressing: a
  barrier separates a draw from earlier draws when it samples what they
  write, writes what they (or a CLUT load) read, or writes what they write
  through another layout. A draw sampling its own target runs on one
  worker, row by row, as the reference order requires.
- Readback and local transfers drain the pool; presentation queues a VRAM
  snapshot and waits for it without blocking the EE thread; FINISH no
  longer waits.
- Each draw records the host FP rounding mode it was submitted with:
  PATH1 draws are kicked from inside the VU1 interpreter (FE_TOWARDZERO),
  so rasterizing them on another thread must restore it.
- PS2X_GS_LOCKSTEP=1 waits for every batch (testing aid).

Verified with a recorded KFIV session (1.86M primitives, title screen to
gameplay) replayed through this and the previous backend: VRAM identical
at all 2111 sync points, transfer state identical after every upload;
the same trace rasterizes about 6x faster in wall time on 8 cores.
